//? Title: Pre-Activation ResNet: Signed Identity Paths Through Complete Depth
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A shortcut cannot carry every signed signal unchanged if a ReLU follows each addition.
Full pre-activation units move BN/ReLU before the filters and leave the sum ungated. We
verify signed values and derivatives through fifty identity units, inspect the projection
exception, then train a complete half-width 164-layer tensor network with visible Adam updates.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.3_residual_and_dense_families/preactivation_resnet.cpp`.

> The method

An equal-width stride-one unit is y=x+F(BN/ReLU(x)), with further preactivation before
each weight layer. There is no nonlinearity after addition. Its bottleneck uses 1×1/3×3/1×1,
with inner width w and outer width 4w. Across identity-shortcut units,

$$
x_L=x_l+\sum_{i=l}^{L-1}F_i(x_i),\qquad
\frac{\partial x_{i+1}}{\partial x_i}=I+J_{F_i}.
$$
\alttext(The raw shortcut retains an identity contribution while learned branches add corrections.)

Setting every final branch kernel to zero gives exact signed identity values and unit input
derivatives through fifty units. This statement includes negative inputs, unlike a post-add
ReLU block. It is an algebraic diagnostic, not proof that arbitrary learned branches cannot
cancel or amplify gradients. When channels/stride change, the projection consumes the first
BN/ReLU result; it is not a raw identity. A controlled projection checks that exception.
A positive-input nonzero branch checks one derivative by central differences away from gates.

The released CIFAR bottleneck layout has depth 9n+2: a raw 3×3 stem, three n-block stages,
final BN/ReLU, global mean and a linear classifier. Base width 16 gives outer stage widths
64/128/256 at 32/16/8 pixels. Depths 164 and 1001 use n=18 and 111, respectively, with
1703258/10327706 learned scalars and 24224/149216 moving scalars. Projection shortcuts are
excluded from the depth name. A width-w bottleneck branch with outer width 4w costs 17w²
kernel weights at unchanged width, plus BN; work sums spatial convolution costs across units.
The full forward checks omit backward storage; quarter-width graphs separately differentiate
every parameter tensor with the complete 164/1001 depths retained.

Convolutions are bias-free with symmetric spatial padding. He-uniform fan-in initialization
differs from the release's He-normal fan-out recipe. BN learns gamma/beta with epsilon 1e-5;
training output uses batch population variance, while eager moving updates use unbiased
variance. Parameters and moving moments belong in separate stores. There is no dropout.

The dataset fit keeps depth 164 at base width eight (half width), with original 32×32 CIFAR-10
images. It shows five epochs, batch sixteen, Adam(.001), explicit kernel L2=.0001/2 and independent
padded crops/flips. The fixed subsets are 64 fit/32 validation/32 official-test rows. The
lowest validation NLL selects paired parameter/moving-state checkpoints before the test pass.
Cached CIFAR-10 is required; this bounded CPU run is not the paper's long SGD schedule.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/preactivation_resnet_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/preactivation_resnet_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/preactivation_resnet_concept.tikz > build/preactivation_resnet_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/preactivation_resnet_concept.svg build/preactivation_resnet_concept.dvi > build/preactivation_resnet_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
